      "hints": [
        "Differentiate again: $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = -2x^{-3/2} = -\\frac{2}{x^{3/2}}$.",
        "Evaluate at $x = 4$: $-\\frac{1}{4} < 0 \\implies$ Maximum."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Differentiate $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 4x^{-1/2} - 2$ a second time:\n$$\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 4\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)x^{-\\frac{3}{2}} = -2x^{-\\frac{3}{2}} = -\\frac{2}{x^{3/2}}$$",
        "Evaluate at the stationary point $x = 4$:\n$$\\left.\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}\\right|_{x=4} = -\\frac{2}{4^{3/2}} = -\\frac{2}{8} = -\\frac{1}{4} < 0$$\nSince the second derivative is negative, the stationary point $(4, 8)$ is a **maximum**."
      ],
